   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Density-Adaptive Rank Space Neighbor Extraction from Multidimensional Data Using Boundary-Corrected Hyper Windows</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Density-Adaptive Rank Space Neighbor Extraction from Multidimensional Data Using Boundary-Corrected Hyper Windows</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>nearest-neighbor search; rank-space transformation; adaptive window selection; density-aware classification; k-nearest neighbors; geometric neighbor extraction</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">k-nearest-neighbor (k-NN) methods remain fundamental to pattern recognition</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">k-nearest-neighbor (k-NN) methods remain fundamental to pattern recognition</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">classification</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">classification</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and image analysis because they are simple</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and image analysis because they are simple</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">non-parametric</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">non-parametric</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and effective for nonlinear decision structures. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and effective for nonlinear decision structures. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">k-NN methods are constrained by two related limitations: explicit pairwise distance computation and reliance on fixed neighborhood parameters</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">k-NN methods are constrained by two related limitations: explicit pairwise distance computation and reliance on fixed neighborhood parameters</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">most notably the parameter k in k-NN and the fixed window-size parameter Diff in Fast Geometrical Extraction of Nearest Neighbors (FGENN). FGENN introduced a distance-free method by transforming data into rank space and extracting nearest neighbors through a geometric hyper-window rather than metric-distance ordering</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">most notably the parameter k in k-NN and the fixed window-size parameter Diff in Fast Geometrical Extraction of Nearest Neighbors (FGENN). FGENN introduced a distance-free method by transforming data into rank space and extracting nearest neighbors through a geometric hyper-window rather than metric-distance ordering</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but its fixed window size does not adapt to local density variation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but its fixed window size does not adapt to local density variation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">causing over-selection in dense regions and under-selection in sparse or boundary regions. In this paper</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">causing over-selection in dense regions and under-selection in sparse or boundary regions. In this paper</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">we propose Density-Adaptive Rank-Space Neighbor Extraction using Boundary-Corrected Hyper-windows (DARNE)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">we propose Density-Adaptive Rank-Space Neighbor Extraction using Boundary-Corrected Hyper-windows (DARNE)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">an adaptive rank-space neighbor-extraction and geometric neighborhood-filtering mechanism that generalizes FGENN</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">an adaptive rank-space neighbor-extraction and geometric neighborhood-filtering mechanism that generalizes FGENN</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">where its fixed window and exhaustive sweep are replaced using an adaptive window size governed by a two-stage local-to-global occupancy-density control law. DARNE outputs ordinal rank neighborhoods in two steps: Stage 1 checks the local rank-lattice occupancy of the query neighborhood by using boundary-corrected hyper-window volumes; Stage 2 analytically scales the window</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">where its fixed window and exhaustive sweep are replaced using an adaptive window size governed by a two-stage local-to-global occupancy-density control law. DARNE outputs ordinal rank neighborhoods in two steps: Stage 1 checks the local rank-lattice occupancy of the query neighborhood by using boundary-corrected hyper-window volumes; Stage 2 analytically scales the window</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">based on the local-to-global density ratio</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">based on the local-to-global density ratio</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">without calculating pairwise Euclidean distances. DARNE also outperforms fixed-window FGENN in query latency for all benchmarks and brute-force k-NN variants on the IRIS</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">without calculating pairwise Euclidean distances. DARNE also outperforms fixed-window FGENN in query latency for all benchmarks and brute-force k-NN variants on the IRIS</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">SONAR and Covertype datasets</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">SONAR and Covertype datasets</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">achieving speedups of 2.78$\times $</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">achieving speedups of 2.78$\times $</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">11.55$\times $ and 21.14$\times $. DARNE achieved accuracies of 97.78%</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">11.55$\times $ and 21.14$\times $. DARNE achieved accuracies of 97.78%</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">94.09%</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">94.09%</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and 69.12% on the IRIS</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and 69.12% on the IRIS</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">SONAR and Covertype datasets</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">SONAR and Covertype datasets</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">respectively</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">respectively</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">at a lower computational cost for queries relative to existing methods. Together</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">at a lower computational cost for queries relative to existing methods. Together</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">these results demonstrate that two-stage density-adaptive windowing in rank space is a reproducible and computationally scalable neighborhood-filtering framework for a wide range of multi-dimensional classification and hashing benchmarks.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">these results demonstrate that two-stage density-adaptive windowing in rank space is a reproducible and computationally scalable neighborhood-filtering framework for a wide range of multi-dimensional classification and hashing benchmarks.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
